#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119974 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d87cbd6-7c9b-5f31-871a-b21000755ef0" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Positive Number Squared"}] :question/id #uuid "e16e7b36-a5fe-5ccd-bb4c-008a01afd5cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13013" :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{15^2}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{15^2} = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119975 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c66352-91eb-5859-a346-0974b3b644ff" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square"}] :question/id #uuid "998557f9-f4d0-594f-ad52-5fa27a73440f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13014" :question/problem "Calculate $\\sqrt{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{36},$ we express $36$ as a perfect square: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6\\cdot 6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{6}^{2}} & = \\\\ 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5aec46-708e-5de8-ad56-ce0c2a711fe2" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "7684ddd0-1e24-57e4-a486-4cdb021a68bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13015" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $9$ and $64$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n9=3^2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n64 = 8^2.\n$$\n\nSo, to work out $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}},$ we express $\\dfrac{9}{64}$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{8\\cdot 8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e3ad2c-3335-5bdd-b7ee-8abcfa4c6951" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Square Root Is a Real Number"}] :question/id #uuid "35769a1e-b922-5c68-a4ca-269526a692b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13016" :question/problem "Which of the following square roots are real numbers?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{0}$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{1.5}$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{-4}$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a negative number is not a real number, while the square root of a non-negative number is a real number.\n\nLet's look at each square root in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{0}$ is the square root of $0,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{0}$ is a real number. In fact, since $0 = 0^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{0} = \\sqrt{0^2} = 0.\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is the square root of $1.5,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{-4}$ is the square root of $-4,$ which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-4}$ is not a real number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119975 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c66352-91eb-5859-a346-0974b3b644ff" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407c3be5-1100-53f0-8300-5734353a8307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446588} {:answer/id #uuid "587a24e8-722c-52c7-9af8-875ecad125af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446591} {:answer/id #